{"name":"Acorn Nut","hex":"#d48948","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acorn nut","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A closed nut having a dome-like cap over the rear protruding part of the engaged screw or bolt."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acorn_nut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acorn nut","blocks":[{"text":"An acorn nut, also referred to as crown hex nut, blind nut, cap nut, domed cap nut, or dome nut (UK), is a nut that has a domed end on one side. When used together with a threaded fastener with an external male thread, the domed end encloses the external thread, either to protect the thread or to protect nearby objects from contact with the thread. In addition, the dome gives a more finished appearance."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Domed_cap_nut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn_nut"},{"title":"Nut (hardware)","blocks":[{"text":"A nut is a type of fastener with a threaded hole. Nuts are almost always used in conjunction with a mating bolt to fasten multiple parts together. The two partners are kept together by a combination of their threads' friction with slight elastic deformation, a slight stretching of the bolt, and compression of the parts to be held together."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/M4_Inbusschraube_focusstacked.jpg/330px-M4_Inbusschraube_focusstacked.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(hardware)"},{"title":"Nut (fruit)","blocks":[{"text":"A nut is a fruit consisting of a hard or tough nutshell protecting a single seed. In botany, the use of the term nut implies that the shell does not open to release the seed."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(fruit)"},{"title":"Acorn","blocks":[{"text":"The acorn is the nut of the oaks and their close relatives ; alternatively known as glans. It usually contains a seedling surrounded by two cotyledons, enclosed in a tough shell known as the pericarp, and borne in a cup-shaped cupule. Acorns take between 5 and 24 months to mature; see the list of Quercus species for details of oak classification, in which acorn morphology and phenology are important factors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG/500px-SydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG/1280px-SydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG/500px-SydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nuts come in many sizes. This one is part of the Sydney Harbour Bridge.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASydneyHarbourBridgeNutMilsonsPoint.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Quergewindebolzen_QD-M6-7%2C5-13x16%2C2.jpg/500px-Quergewindebolzen_QD-M6-7%2C5-13x16%2C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Quergewindebolzen_QD-M6-7%2C5-13x16%2C2.jpg/500px-Quergewindebolzen_QD-M6-7%2C5-13x16%2C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuergewindebolzen_QD-M6-7%2C5-13x16%2C2.j